        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-c5dc6576178b84bb\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite explicitly avows the great extent of her love for Armand. The utterance responds to his oath and is emotionally intense, but it does not itself undertake or renew a commitment. Her embrace, tears, and emphatic delivery are adequately treated as context for a passionate assertion; they do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The immediate, embodied response to Armand's oath creates slight pressure toward E, but the phrasing is a coherent explanatory avowal rather than a depicted involuntary or reflex-like discharge.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Marguerite's directly quoted speech is embedded in Armand's retrospective narration and presented as something she would say on repeated occasions.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite says this after asking Armand never to leave her and scrutinizing the sincerity of his oath. Her avowal explains the stakes of her fear: she experiences their shared life as transformative and dreads abandonment and a return to her former existence. The surrounding embrace and concealment of her face intensify the sincerity and vulnerability of the declaration without changing its dominant truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“C'est que tu ne sais pas combien je t'aime!”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense statement strongly supports T as an avowal of the extent of Marguerite's love.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite asks, “Dis-moi donc que tu ne me quitteras jamais,” and Armand answers, “Je te le jure!”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance follows Armand's commitment and functions primarily as Marguerite's explanation of why his reassurance matters, rather than as her own undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She looks for sincerity in his eyes, throws herself into his arms, and hides her head against his chest before speaking.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes emotional vulnerability and passionate delivery, but does not by itself show that the words have independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration describes their love as “violent, confiant et partagé” and records Marguerite's fear that Armand may later abandon her because of her past.\",\"supports\":\"The wider relationship context corroborates a serious loving state and explains the anxious function of the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense “je t'aime” in Chapter XVIII of Alexandre Dumas fils's La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the linguistic form, location, and identity of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its emotional intensity and reassuring context can be described without adding E, while the commitment belongs to Armand's preceding oath rather than Marguerite's love declaration. No residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
